[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Quaternions & Rotation Mechanics in Unity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Rotations in game development can be notoriously tricky. While Euler angles (Vector3) are intuitive for humans, they suffer from severe mathematical limitations in software, such as Gimbal Lock. Unity uses Quaternions under the hood to calculate and store rotations safely and efficiently."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is a Quaternion?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Quaternion is a four-dimensional mathematical construct consisting of one real component (w) and three imaginary components (x, y, z). Unlike matrices or Euler angles, quaternions represent a rotation around an arbitrary axis by a specific angle. They eliminate Gimbal Lock and allow smooth interpolation (Slerp) between rotations."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Quaternion Methods in Unity"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Quaternion.identity: Represents 'no rotation' (equivalent to Vector3.zero)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Quaternion.Euler(Vector3 euler): Converts standard human-readable Euler angles into a Quaternion."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Quaternion.LookRotation(Vector3 forward): Creates a rotation with the specified forward vector."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Quaternion.Slerp(Quaternion a, Quaternion b, float t): Smoothly interpolates between rotation a and b."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Smoothly Rotating Towards a Target"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;

public class RotateTowardsTarget : MonoBehaviour
{
    public Transform target;
    public float rotationSpeed = 5.0f;

    void Update()
    {
        if (target == null) return;

        Vector3 direction = target.position - transform.position;
        Quaternion targetRotation = Quaternion.LookRotation(direction);

        transform.rotation = Quaternion.Slerp(transform.rotation, targetRotation, rotationSpeed * Time.deltaTime);
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Never modify individual x, y, z, or w components of a Quaternion manually unless you are an advanced graphics programmer."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use multiplication to combine rotations: Quaternion combined = rotationA * rotationB;"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer Quaternion.Slerp or Quaternion.RotateTowards over direct assignments for smoothing out camera movements and AI turning."}]
